From 78bedbe6845471b380f0c66191f082ce2ba19c79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 19:21:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 114/114] drm/vino: bind the display function, and place docks
 by family

The driver matched two product IDs and took everything else -- endpoints,
strip geometry, connector count, link limits -- from a table keyed by those
IDs. That cannot describe hardware nobody has tested, and a dock missing
from the table is not merely unsupported but invisible: it is never offered
to the driver at all.

Bind the *function* instead. DisplayLink's own udev rules match vendor 17e9
and then trigger on the interface, with no product test anywhere; reverse
engineering the wire protocol found the same split independently, where
interface protocol 0x03 is a DL3 display function and 0x00 is the older udl
hardware, which is a different driver's problem. Matching class 0xff /
subclass 0 / protocol 0x03 plus the DFU interface offers every DisplayLink
dock to the driver, including ones that do not exist yet.

What makes that acceptable is that the dock can be asked what it is. The
identity descriptor read for the firmware check names the platform, so use
it to select the profile too, and decline by name when it names a family
this driver cannot drive -- a guessed profile is worse than no driver,
because the way a dock rejects a guess is to reset itself. A device whose
identity cannot be *read* falls back to a small product-ID quirk table, so
a transient descriptor failure does not cost a known dock its displays.

The connector count now follows the video endpoints the device actually
exposes, bounded by the profile, so a dock in a known family with fewer
outputs is driven with the outputs it has rather than failing endpoint
resolution outright.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
